{"name":"Electric Energy","hex":"#c9e423","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"electric energy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The potential energy of an electric charge in an electric field, or of an electric current in a magnetic field.","Electrical energy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/electric_energy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Electrical energy","blocks":[{"text":"Electrical energy is the energy transferred as electric charges move between points with different electric potential, that is, as they move across a potential difference. As electric potential is lost or gained, work is done changing the energy of some system. The amount of work in joules is given by the product of the charge that has moved, in coulombs, and the potential difference that has been crossed, in volts."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrical_energy","wikidata":"Q206799"},{"title":"Electricity","blocks":[{"text":"Electricity is the set of physical phenomena associated with the presence and motion of matter possessing an electric charge. Electricity is related to magnetism, both being part of the phenomenon of electromagnetism, as described by Maxwell's equations. Common phenomena are related to electricity, including lightning, static electricity, electric heating, electric discharges and many others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/London_MMB_%C2%BB1E6_Lightning.jpg/330px-London_MMB_%C2%BB1E6_Lightning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity","wikidata":"Q12725"},{"title":"Energy","blocks":[{"text":"Energy is the quantitative property that is transferred to a body or to a physical system, recognizable in the capacity to do work and in the form of heat and light. Energy is a conserved quantity – the law of conservation of energy states that the total energy of an isolated system remains constant, although it can be converted in form. The unit of measurement for energy in the International System of Units (SI) is the joule (J)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Plasma_globe_60th.jpg/330px-Plasma_globe_60th.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy","wikidata":"Q11379"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Thales.jpg/330px-Thales.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Thales.jpg/330px-Thales.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A fictional portrait of Thales, the earliest known researcher into electricity","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThales.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg/500px-Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg/1280px-Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg/500px-Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Benjamin Franklin conducted extensive research on electricity in the 18th century, as documented by Joseph Priestley (1767) History and Present Status of Electricity, with whom Franklin carried on extended correspondence.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFranklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/M_Faraday_Th_Phillips_oil_1842.jpg/500px-M_Faraday_Th_Phillips_oil_1842.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.or